\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Let\'92s start with a few general questions. How old are you, where do you live, how long have you lived there, and what do you do for a living? Also, what are you prioritizing in your life right now\'97self-development, relationships, career?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Hi, I\'92m Toma. I\'92m 37 and I live in San Francisco. I work in tech\'97strategy and analytics. Career is definitely my priority.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  So most of your time is dedicated to your career right now?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes, though I\'92m currently unemployed\'97I got laid off. I had a short catch-up job and now I\'92m interviewing, so job search is my top priority. I\'92m also very into music.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What do your days look like besides the job search? What do you do casually, on days off?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Lots of walking and hiking. And music\'97music is a huge part of my life. I\'92m not a musician, but I\'92m always around music; I couldn\'92t live without it. I used to go to a lot of shows\'97less lately because of the job search and because I started a new relationship about six months ago.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Can you tell us a bit more about that relationship? It started around six months ago and it\'92s developing?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes\'97it\'92s pretty new, and very different from what I\'92ve had before. I\'92m literally at his place right now.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What makes it feel different?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  I tend to have an anxious attachment style. In the past I\'92d go for people who were a bit unavailable and try to earn their attention\'97that chase would become the struggle. With him it\'92s different. At first I said I wasn\'92t ready, that I was focused on my career. He said, \'93Okay\'97have your space, do what you need. I just want to be there for you.\'94 Little by little I found myself spending more time with him. He kind of won me over with love, kindness, and support.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  So your anxious attachment is less activated in this relationship?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  He\'92s aware of it\'97because I told him\'97and he tries to show up in ways that help. For example, today he said he\'92d text and then got delayed. I worried, and he just came home to be with me and said, \'93I know I was supposed to be there for you.\'94 He\'92s doing his best to be present, knowing I have anxiety around being ignored. It\'92s new, different\'97kind of beautiful.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  It really is. Being able to name what you need and having someone receive it respectfully\'97and even help\'97is such a gift.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Are you familiar with the attachment style theory?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  How did you first learn about it or realize how it applies to you?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  I kept finding myself attracted to avoidant types. The more unavailable someone was, the more interested I became. It\'92s like a little \'93moment syndrome\'94 \'97 I\'92d create an idealized image of someone and start chasing it.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  So your own experience led you to explore it, to figure out what was happening?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Exactly. My therapist and I worked on it when I was still in therapy.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Are you still in therapy now?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Not right now \'97 therapy in the U.S. is expensive and I lost my health insurance after being laid off. But I used to work with someone regularly.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Let\'92s talk a bit more about the anxiety itself. How often do you experience it \'97 especially in relationships?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  It really depends on the type of relationship. When there\'92s trust and connection, the anxiety is minimal. But when that\'92s missing, the brain starts spiraling. When something you expect doesn\'92t happen, insecurity and lack of trust feed each other.\
When I feel confident in my partner, it happens less. But when confidence is gone, fear of being unseen or abandoned kicks in \'97 and that\'92s when anxiety shows up.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  You mentioned that it depends on the kind of relationship. Can you tell us when it usually appears most \'97 maybe at the beginning, when things are undefined?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Definitely at the beginning, but it can also happen later. For example, I dated someone for three and a half years. He\'92d go out for the night and text, \'93I\'92ll be home by midnight,\'94 but then it\'92s 3 a.m. and nothing. I\'92d message, \'93Hey, is everything okay?\'94 and he\'92d reply, \'93Yeah, just out with friends.\'94\
I didn\'92t need constant updates \'97 I just wanted to know he was safe. But he\'92d say, \'93Should I tell you my every step?\'94 I\'92d answer, \'93No, I just want to feel connected.\'94 For me, communication is care.\
At first I thought I was wrong for needing that. I\'92d guilt myself \'97 \'93I\'92m too needy, I should be cooler.\'94 But then I realized: what I\'92m asking for isn\'92t too much. It\'92s just not being met.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  So you communicate openly about it, instead of holding it in and blaming yourself?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes, though in some relationships I started changing myself \'97 becoming smaller, quieter, trying not to \'93bother.\'94 Over time I realized that\'92s not healthy. Asking for connection isn\'92t too much; silence isn\'92t balance.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  I totally relate to that. I also used to act like, \'93I\'92m chill, I don\'92t care, you can do whatever,\'94 but that wasn\'92t true. I thought that was the only way someone would accept and love me. Deep down I was afraid that if I expressed my needs, I\'92d be rejected.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes, exactly.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  That\'92s actually one of the intentions behind the practices we\'92re creating \'97 to help people reconnect with what\'92s real for them.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Right. To offer a kind of navigation \'97 not to tell someone what\'92s right or wrong, but to help them see what 
\f2\i feels
\f1\i0  true for them. Because so often we\'92re lost in guilt, fear, and confusion \'97 unsure if we should act this way or that.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yeah.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Our goal is to help people reach that secure, grounded place where they can sense, \'93This is good for me,\'94 or, \'93This isn\'92t,\'94 and trust that.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  That\'92s about knowing your own value.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Exactly. It\'92s something you have to experience, not just read about. But having support makes it faster and safer \'97 so you don\'92t keep going in circles.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  And it\'92s also about balance. We can\'92t expect our partner to carry all the responsibility for our attachment patterns \'97 that wouldn\'92t be honest. They can support us, but the work is still ours.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Totally. No one\'92s perfect \'97 it\'92s two imperfect people choosing each other. Arguments will happen. What matters is 
\f2\i what we do
\f1\i0  with them: whether we shut down, or listen and try to understand.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  For me, communication is everything. You don\'92t have to agree to understand. I might not share your opinion, but I\'92ll try to see where you\'92re coming from. That mutual willingness \'97 to listen, to find common ground \'97 that\'92s what keeps connection alive.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  If we go a bit back \'97 when your anxiety used to be stronger \'97 how did it feel for you? Was it more in the body, or in the mind?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  It took me a long time to even get to therapy. My anxiety used to be really physical. I\'92d feel adrenaline rushes, my chest tightening, heart racing \'97 like I couldn\'92t breathe properly. Sometimes I\'92d feel dizzy or nauseous. It was hard to sleep.\
Even now, the mental part is easier \'97 I can recognize what\'92s happening and catch myself \'97 but the body still reacts. When that adrenaline wave hits, I just have to ride it out, breathe through it, and let it pass.\
During lockdown it got worse. Everything was closed, no job, so much uncertainty. I forced myself to go outside and walk every day. At first just around the block, then longer. I also started taking pictures \'97 little photo challenges to shift focus.\
Like, one day I\'92d look for everything green \'97 traffic lights, doors, trees. It became a small daily ritual that made me notice beauty again. Creativity helped me cope.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Oh, I did something similar once \'97 I searched for blue colors around me.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes! It\'92s about shifting focus. You start to notice small, beautiful things and it pulls you out of the spiral.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Happiness is like a muscle \'97 you can train it.\

\f0\b Zoom user:
\f1\b0  After those walks, did your body feel calmer?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes. Especially when I\'92d listen to podcasts \'97 it became a kind of moving meditation. Of course, some days nothing worked and I just had to wait it out. But overall, it helped a lot.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  That\'92s really great \'97 you found a way to release the stress through the body. Walking, moving, engaging the senses \'97 all that helps the hormones balance and the nervous system settle. It\'92s amazing work you\'92ve done.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Thank you.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  I have a question about communication. When something triggers you in a relationship \'97 for example, a conflict or misunderstanding \'97 how do you usually handle it? Do you first process it on your own, or do you communicate right away?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  It depends on the situation. If I\'92m relatively calm, I can talk about it immediately. But if it\'92s a strong emotional moment, I need some time to cool down first \'97 to sit with it and understand what\'92s happening. After that, I always come back to talk.\
For me, communication is essential. I really try to understand the other person, not just expect them to understand me. I put myself in their shoes, listen, and try to see their side.\
But sometimes it\'92s frustrating \'97 when I\'92m trying to explain how I feel and they still don\'92t get it. I keep repeating, hoping to be heard, and then I realize I sound like a broken record. I\'92m not trying to nag \'97 I just want connection.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  In my current relationship, it\'92s very different. We both get triggered sometimes, but we talk it through. Even if we don\'92t agree, we listen to each other. I may not get immediate understanding, but I feel heard \'97 and that changes everything.\
Being heard doesn\'92t always mean being agreed with, but it gives such relief. When you\'92re not heard, when you keep trying and get pushed away \'97 that\'92s incredibly painful.\

\f0\b Zoom user:
\f1\b0  You mentioned you used to have many episodes of anxiety. How deep were they? For example, I\'92ve had panic attacks where I completely lost control. Did you experience something similar?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  I\'92m sorry you went through that \'97 it\'92s terrifying. I didn\'92t have full-blown panic attacks, but I had severe anxiety attacks. Everything would start spinning, my body frozen and restless at the same time. I couldn\'92t move or think; I just wanted to escape.\
Physically it was awful \'97 my chest tight, heart racing, body buzzing with adrenaline. I felt trapped inside my own body. Thankfully, that happens much less often now, but it still comes back sometimes.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  It takes a lot of strength and energy to go through that. It\'92s really intense, and I can imagine how much work it takes to manage and recover.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes, definitely.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What\'92s your relationship with breathwork or meditation? Do you practice anything like that?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Meditation is hard for me. I\'92ve tried many times, but my ADHD brain just keeps running. What helps more are things that keep my body engaged \'97 walking, cooking, doing something with my hands or legs. That calms my mind.\
When I walk, I listen to something light \'97 a podcast or music \'97 and let myself wander. It distracts my mind just enough to slow down. Breathwork helps too, especially when I try to calm myself.\
I know simple techniques like box breathing \'97 inhale, hold, exhale, hold \'97 and I use them to fall asleep or when I feel anxious. I don\'92t do it as a routine, more when needed.\
But yoga helped the most. Before the layoff, I went to slow-flow candlelight yoga \'97 not the intense kind, but gentle stretching with breath awareness. It reminded me to slow down and just 
\f2\i be
\f1\i0  in the moment.\
It\'92s powerful when someone simply guides you to breathe and slow down. I think practices like that are amazing, as long as you can get yourself there mentally. And if you can\'92t yet, that\'92s okay too.\
That\'92s why I really love your idea \'97 to have different levels of practice: short, medium, long. It\'92s like gentle onboarding \'97 try it, see how it feels. Maybe not right away, but eventually something clicks.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What would make you feel drawn to a product like this? And what would turn you off?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  I\'92ve tried apps like 
\f2\i Headspace
\f1\i0 . At first, it was nice, but then it became too repetitive \'97 same tone, same words, same flow. It didn\'92t feel engaging.\
Because of my ADHD, I need something that feels dynamic. When it\'92s the same every day, my mind tunes out.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  So you\'92d prefer more variety, right?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Exactly \'97 something that keeps me curious.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What about spirituality? How do you relate to it?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  I\'92m familiar with it, but I lean more toward a scientific or grounded approach. Spiritual language can lose me \'97 I connect better when it\'92s logical and real-world.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  So more science-based communication, less esoteric?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes, that\'92s what feels right for me.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Let\'92s talk about the product itself. What kind of format would attract you more \'97 a step-by-step course or something flexible where you can choose practices depending on your mood or state?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  For people who are new to this kind of work, a structured path would help \'97 something like \'9310 days, 10 practices.\'94 You could even gamify it a bit. When you have too many options, it\'92s easy to get lost and not know where to start.\
If you make it fun \'97 like \'93don\'92t break your streak\'94 or \'93unlock the next level\'94 \'97 it keeps people engaged and curious to see what\'92s next. Every day something slightly different: maybe a new theme, one minute longer, a new insight. That would make me want to continue.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  So gamification works for you \'97 making the process more playful?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Definitely. And having options too \'97 like a guided mode for beginners and a self-guided one for people who already know what they\'92re doing. Someone might just want to pick a practice based on their current mood \'97 that flexibility would be great.\

\f0\b Zoom user:
\f1\b0  Good idea.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  You mentioned 
\f2\i Headspace
\f1\i0 . Have you tried any other apps or online courses like that?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Not many. I tried 
\f2\i Headspace
\f1\i0  because my job offered it as a benefit. I sometimes subscribe to apps for a month, test them, and then lose interest \'97 that\'92s just my ADHD brain. I get super into something, then drop it a few weeks later.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Where do you usually find out about such products?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Mostly social media \'97 Instagram, sometimes TikTok. That\'92s how most people hear about them these days.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  What usually catches your attention \'97 visuals, messaging, storytelling?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  A mix of both. Some ads are flashy and manipulative \'97 they get your attention but feel fake. When it looks too pushy \'97 all caps, golden \'93LIMITED OFFER\'94 banners \'97 it\'92s a red flag.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Aggressive marketing?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Exactly. Also, those endless \'93personality quizzes\'94 that lead to a paywall at the end \'97 hate that. I\'92d rather see something honest and authentic.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Maybe something more subtle \'97 like someone sharing their real experience: \'93I tried this, and here\'92s what changed for me.\'94\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Yes. That feels trustworthy. People are tired of fake enthusiasm \'97 they want honesty.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Do you follow any bloggers or creators you actually trust when they recommend something?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Not really \'93influencers.\'94 I follow people who feel genuine \'97 smaller creators, maybe 300 followers \'97 but when they say something that speaks to my soul, I pay attention. It feels human.\
These days, though, social media marketing is losing trust. Everyone\'92s selling something. If it\'92s integrated naturally into a story, that\'92s fine, but the moment it feels forced \'97 people pull away.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Yes, authenticity is key.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Exactly. It\'92s better when it feels like a conversation, not a sales pitch.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  By the way, I actually work in user engagement \'97 not marketing exactly, but connecting users with content they enjoy. I analyze how people interact with digital products and what keeps them coming back.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Oh, that\'92s great! Can you share a bit more about what you do?\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Sure. My role is to study engagement \'97 understanding how to create meaningful connections between users and what they consume. Basically, how to make experiences more personal and motivating.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  That\'92s very close to what we\'92re doing \'97 building emotional connection through product experience.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Exactly. And psychology plays a huge role. Especially now, with AI being used for self-help, there\'92s a fine line between being supportive and pretending to be a therapist.\
You don\'92t want to replace therapy \'97 you want to be a companion. Someone saying, \'93I see you, I get you, and I\'92m here.\'94 That could be your message.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Yes \'97 we want to create something useful, not just profitable.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Then focus on your 
\f2\i point of view
\f1\i0 . In marketing, that\'92s what makes a product stand out. What are you really selling \'97 peace, clarity, self-trust? Define that.\
If you call it just \'93a meditation app,\'94 you\'92ll get lost among thousands. But if your message is \'93we\'92re not therapists, we\'92re your support system,\'94 that\'92s unique and powerful.\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  That\'92s a great point \'97 thank you. We\'92ll definitely test and refine that.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  You\'92re doing something important \'97 the world needs it. Just be mindful of how you position it so it doesn\'92t drown in the noise.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  Thank you so much, Toma. This was really valuable \'97 and you gave us a lot to think about.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Thank you for having me. Sorry if I was a bit all over the place!\

\f0\b Kseniya Yakovleva:
\f1\b0  Not at all \'97 it was wonderful. Thank you for being open and honest.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  You\'92re very welcome. And if I can help with user engagement advice later \'97 just reach out.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  We definitely will.\

\f0\b Toma:
\f1\b0  Great. Take care, guys.\

\f0\b Maria Sokolova:
\f1\b0  You too. Bye!\
}